<script>
'use strict';

/* ============================================================
   QUESTION BANK
   type: 'mc' | 'wr'
   channel: matches chip data-ch
   options (MC): array of {text, correct, why}
   concepts (WR): array of {label, terms:[...]}
   answer (WR): full model answer HTML string
============================================================ */
const ALL_QUESTIONS = [

// ── RETURNS & ANNUALIZATION ─────────────────────────────────

{id:'mc-ann-sqrt', type:'mc', channel:'returns', channel_label:'Returns & Annualization',
 text:`<code>analyze_equity.py:20</code> computes annualized volatility as:
<pre class="snippet"><span class="hi">vol</span> = <span class="fn">float</span>(ret.<span class="fn">std</span>()) * math.<span class="fn">sqrt</span>(ann)   <span class="cm"># ann = 252</span></pre>
Why does this multiply by <strong>√252</strong> rather than <strong>×252</strong>?`,
 options:[
   {text:'Variance adds linearly across independent periods, so standard deviation scales by the square root of the number of periods.',correct:true,
    why:'Var(annual) = 252 × Var(daily) → σ_annual = σ_daily × √252. The square-root rule follows from variance additivity.'},
   {text:'The square root is a convention agreed upon by industry regulators; there is no deeper mathematical reason.',correct:false,
    why:'Wrong — the √252 rule is derived from the variance additivity property of independent returns, not arbitrary convention.'},
   {text:'pandas .std() uses ddof=1 which introduces a √n bias that must be corrected by dividing by √(n/ann).',correct:false,
    why:'Wrong — Bessel\'s correction (ddof=1) divides by n-1 for unbiasedness; it doesn\'t change the annualization exponent.'},
   {text:'Returns are bounded in [-1,+∞) which compresses their distribution, requiring a root transform to annualize fairly.',correct:false,
    why:'Wrong — the √n scaling comes from variance additivity, not from the asymmetric support of simple returns.'},
 ]},

{id:'mc-shift', type:'mc', channel:'returns', channel_label:'Returns & Annualization',
 text:`In <code>paper_trading_sim.py:68</code>:
<pre class="snippet">out[<span class="hi">"fwd_ret_1d"</span>] = out[<span class="hi">"ret_1d"</span>].<span class="fn">shift</span>(<span class="num">-1</span>)</pre>
What is the purpose of <code>.shift(-1)</code> here?`,
 options:[
   {text:'To align tomorrow\'s return with today\'s features, so the model trained on row t predicts the return that will be realized on day t+1.',correct:true,
    why:'shift(-1) moves values one step backward in time. Row t now contains today\'s features alongside tomorrow\'s return — the target the model should predict.'},
   {text:'To lag returns by one day so that features and target are from the same trading session, preventing look-ahead.',correct:false,
    why:'Incorrect — shift(-1) moves target values backward (earlier), not forward. Shift(+1) would lag. The goal is to attach tomorrow\'s return as today\'s label.'},
   {text:'To compute a 1-period moving average of daily returns for use as a momentum feature.',correct:false,
    why:'Wrong — shift(-1) just shifts index alignment; it performs no averaging. The moving averages use .rolling().mean().'},
   {text:'To convert simple returns to log returns by offsetting the time axis.',correct:false,
    why:'Wrong — the conversion from simple to log returns uses np.log(1 + ret); shift(-1) only changes which date a value is aligned to.'},
 ]},

{id:'wr-log-vs-simple', type:'wr', channel:'returns', channel_label:'Returns & Annualization',
 text:'Explain the key difference between <strong>simple returns</strong> and <strong>log returns</strong>. Which is additive across <em>assets</em>? Which across <em>time</em>? Which does the repo use, and why?',
 concepts:[
   {label:'Simple return formula', terms:['pct_change','(p_t','p_t-1','p_{t-1}','(pt','percent change','pct change']},
   {label:'Log return formula', terms:['ln(','log(','ln ','log return','logarithm']},
   {label:'Cross-asset additivity (simple)', terms:['cross-asset','portfolio return','asset','weight','weighted','assets']},
   {label:'Cross-time additivity (log)', terms:['time','compound','additive across time','sum of log','log-additiv','across period']},
   {label:'Repo uses simple', terms:['pct_change','simple','repo','this repo','paper_trading']},
 ],
 answer:`<h4>Model Answer</h4>
<strong>Simple return:</strong> r_t = (P_t − P_{t-1}) / P_{t-1}, computed in the repo via <code>ret.pct_change()</code>.
<br><strong>Log return:</strong> r_t = ln(P_t / P_{t-1}).
<br><br>
<strong>Additive across assets (simple):</strong> A portfolio return is the weighted sum of individual asset simple returns: r_p = Σ w_i r_i. This works because of how prices combine in a portfolio. Log returns do NOT have this property.
<br><br>
<strong>Additive across time (log):</strong> Log returns sum across periods: multi-period return = Σ ln(P_t/P_{t-1}). Simple returns must be compounded, not summed. Log returns are also symmetric around zero.
<br><br>
<strong>The repo uses simple returns</strong> (<code>pct_change()</code>) as features and as the target (<code>fwd_ret_1d</code>) because the cross-sectional ranking step weights asset returns in a portfolio, which requires simple-return additivity.`
},

// ── RISK METRICS ──────────────────────────────────────────────

{id:'mc-maxdd', type:'mc', channel:'risk', channel_label:'Risk Metrics',
 text:`<code>analyze_equity.py:26</code>:
<pre class="snippet">maxdd = <span class="fn">float</span>((cum / roll_max - <span class="num">1.0</span>).<span class="fn">min</span>())</pre>
What does the <code>.min()</code> call produce?`,
 options:[
   {text:'The most negative value in the drawdown series — i.e., the worst peak-to-trough loss fraction the strategy ever experienced.',correct:true,
    why:'cum/roll_max - 1 ≤ 0 always. The minimum is the maximum drawdown — the single worst drop from any prior peak.'},
   {text:'The date index where the drawdown was largest, expressed as a float.',correct:false,
    why:'Wrong — .min() on a pandas Series of floats returns the minimum float value, not a date index.'},
   {text:'The average drawdown across all periods, which is always negative.',correct:false,
    why:'Wrong — .mean() would give the average; .min() gives the single worst observation.'},
   {text:'A value between 0 and 1 representing the proportion of trading days spent in drawdown.',correct:false,
    why:'Wrong — cum/roll_max - 1 is ≤ 0 everywhere (not bounded to [-1, 0] either); .min() finds the worst single-day value, not a proportion.'},
 ]},

{id:'mc-ddof', type:'mc', channel:'risk', channel_label:'Risk Metrics',
 text:'In <code>analyze_equity.py:20</code>, <code>ret.std()</code> is called without any extra arguments. What does pandas use as the denominator inside the standard deviation calculation?',
 options:[
   {text:'n − 1 (Bessel\'s correction), meaning pandas defaults to ddof=1, producing the unbiased sample standard deviation.',correct:true,
    why:'pandas Series.std() defaults to ddof=1, dividing by n-1. NumPy np.std() defaults to ddof=0 (population std). The difference matters at small n.'},
   {text:'n (the population standard deviation), because financial returns are rarely modeled as a sample from a parent distribution.',correct:false,
    why:'Wrong — pandas defaults to ddof=1 (divide by n-1). It\'s NumPy that defaults to ddof=0. Mixing these is a real source of bugs.'},
   {text:'n − 2, applying a two-sample correction needed when computing both mean and std from the same data.',correct:false,
    why:'Wrong — ddof=2 is used in specific regression residual contexts, not as a pandas default.'},
   {text:'252, the annualization factor, because pandas automatically normalizes financial time series by trading days.',correct:false,
    why:'Wrong — pandas has no special financial-normalization defaults; ddof controls only the degrees-of-freedom correction.'},
 ]},

{id:'wr-sharpe', type:'wr', channel:'risk', channel_label:'Risk Metrics',
 text:'Walk through the Sharpe ratio formula from <code>analyze_equity.py:22–23</code>. Explain what each component measures and what a Sharpe ratio above 2.0 implies.',
 concepts:[
   {label:'Numerator: mean × 252 (expected annual return)', terms:['mean','expected','annuali','252','average return']},
   {label:'Denominator: std × √252 (annualized volatility)', terms:['std','volatil','sqrt','denomin','risk']},
   {label:'Risk-free rate assumed zero', terms:['risk-free','risk free','rf = 0','rf=0','zero rate']},
   {label:'Sharpe > 2 implication', terms:['2','excellent','signal','statistic','unlikely','random','noise']},
 ],
 answer:`<h4>Model Answer</h4>
From <code>analyze_equity.py:22-23</code>:<br>
<code>sharpe = (ret.mean() * 252) / (ret.std() * sqrt(252))</code>
<br><br>
<strong>Numerator:</strong> <code>ret.mean() × 252</code> — the expected annualized return (arithmetic, not geometric). Scales by 252 trading days.
<br><strong>Denominator:</strong> <code>ret.std() × √252</code> — annualized volatility. Uses √252 because variance adds across periods.
<br><strong>Risk-free rate:</strong> The repo assumes r_f = 0. In full form: Sharpe = (E[r_s] − r_f) / σ_s.
<br><br>
<strong>Sharpe > 2.0</strong> means you earn >2 units of excess return per unit of volatility annually. In academic finance, Sharpe > 1 is considered solid; > 2 is excellent; > 3 raises suspicion (could signal overfit or look-ahead). For a live strategy at a hedge fund, a realized > 1.5 over 3+ years is strong.`
},

{id:'wr-maxdd-why', type:'wr', channel:'risk', channel_label:'Risk Metrics',
 text:'Why do investors often focus on <strong>maximum drawdown</strong> as much as (or more than) Sharpe ratio? What psychological and structural factors make it the "career risk" metric?',
 concepts:[
   {label:'Emotional impact of drawdown', terms:['emotion','psych','feel','pain','loss aversion']},
   {label:'Redemptions / client capital', terms:['redempt','client','capital','withdraw','allocator','fund']},
   {label:'Sharpe doesn\'t capture tail/path', terms:['sharpe','path','sequence','tail','distribution','skew']},
   {label:'Definition of max drawdown', terms:['peak','trough','worst','minimum','drawdown','peak-to-trough']},
 ],
 answer:`<h4>Model Answer</h4>
<strong>Maximum drawdown (MaxDD)</strong> is the worst peak-to-trough loss the equity curve ever experienced. Computed as <code>min(equity_t / rolling_peak_t − 1)</code>, always ≤ 0.
<br><br>
<strong>Why it dominates Sharpe in practice:</strong>
<ol style="margin:.5rem 0 .5rem 1.2rem;line-height:1.8">
  <li><strong>Investor psychology:</strong> Clients experience drawdown as real loss, even if unrealized. Loss aversion means a 20% drop hurts more than a 20% gain helps.</li>
  <li><strong>Redemption risk:</strong> A deep drawdown triggers client redemptions, forcing asset sales at the worst time — locking in losses and preventing recovery.</li>
  <li><strong>Career risk:</strong> PMs are fired after large drawdowns; strategies are shut down. A high Sharpe strategy with a 40% max drawdown may not survive long enough to deliver its edge.</li>
  <li><strong>Sharpe is path-blind:</strong> Two strategies can have identical Sharpe but very different drawdown profiles. Sharpe uses only mean and std; it ignores the sequence of returns.</li>
</ol>`
},

