{id:'mc-cagr-apr', type:'mc', channel:'cagr', channel_label:'CAGR vs APR',
 text:'A strategy has a mean daily return of 0.10% and daily volatility of 1.5%. Which statement about its annualized CAGR vs APR_simple is correct?',
 options:[
   {text:'CAGR &lt; APR because compounding reduces the geometric mean relative to the arithmetic mean (volatility drag ≈ σ²/2 per year).',correct:true,
    why:'Annual volatility drag ≈ σ²/2 ≈ (0.015√252)²/2 ≈ (0.238)²/2 ≈ 2.8%. So CAGR ≈ APR − 2.8%. Higher volatility = bigger gap.'},
   {text:'CAGR &gt; APR because compounding causes gains to grow faster than simple addition assumes.',correct:false,
    why:'Wrong — compounding helps if there were no volatility, but volatility drag always makes CAGR ≤ APR. Gains do not compound "faster" than simple arithmetic predicts.'},
   {text:'CAGR = APR whenever the risk-free rate is assumed to be zero, as in analyze_equity.py.',correct:false,
    why:'Wrong — the CAGR/APR gap comes from volatility (Jensen\'s inequality on the log transform), not from the risk-free rate assumption.'},
   {text:'CAGR &lt; APR only when the Sharpe ratio is below 1, because low Sharpe implies the compounding penalty exceeds the return.',correct:false,
