{id:'wr-vol-drag', type:'wr', channel:'cagr', channel_label:'CAGR vs APR',
 text:'Explain <strong>volatility drag</strong> in your own words. What is the approximate formula relating CAGR, APR, and annual volatility σ? If two strategies have the same expected return but different volatility, which compounds faster?',
 concepts:[
   {label:'CAGR vs APR gap', terms:['cagr','apr','gap','difference','less than','≤','compound']},
   {label:'Formula: CAGR ≈ APR − σ²/2', terms:['σ²/2','sigma squared','variance','half','formula','approx']},
   {label:'Higher vol → larger drag', terms:['higher vol','larger gap','more volatility','bigger drag','volatility increases']},
   {label:'Lower volatility compounds faster', terms:['lower vol','less volatility','compounds faster','better','prefer']},
 ],
 answer:`<h4>Model Answer</h4>
<strong>Volatility drag:</strong> Because returns compound geometrically rather than adding arithmetically, randomness always reduces the realized growth rate below the expected arithmetic return.
<br><br>
<strong>Formula:</strong> CAGR ≈ APR − σ²/2<br>
where APR = E[r] × 252 and σ = daily_std × √252. This comes from the log-return expansion: E[ln(1+r)] ≈ μ − σ²/2.
<br><br>
<strong>Example:</strong> APR = 10%, σ = 20% → CAGR ≈ 10% − 4%/2 = 8%.
<br><br>
<strong>Implication:</strong> Two strategies with identical expected returns — the one with lower volatility compounds faster. This is why mean-variance optimization explicitly penalizes variance, and why the Sharpe ratio uses volatility as the denominator.`
},

{id:'wr-cagr-formula', type:'wr', channel:'cagr', channel_label:'CAGR vs APR',
 text:`<code>analyze_equity.py:19</code> computes:
<pre class="snippet">cagr = (final / initial) ** (ann / <span class="fn">max</span>(<span class="num">1.0</span>, n)) - <span class="num">1.0</span></pre>
Why does this formula use <strong>exponentiation</strong> instead of division? And what does <code>max(1.0, n)</code> guard against?`,
 concepts:[
   {label:'Geometric compounding', terms:['compound','geometr','annuali','exponenti','power','year']},
   {label:'The exponent ann/n = 252/n', terms:['252/n','ann/n','exponent','scale','normaliz','ratio']},
   {label:'max(1,n) prevents division by zero', terms:['division by zero','zero','n=0','guard','protect','max']},
   {label:'Different from APR (no exponentiation)', terms:['apr','arithmetic','mean','no exponent','simpler']},
 ],
 answer:`<h4>Model Answer</h4>
<strong>Why exponentiation:</strong> CAGR is the geometrically-compounded annual growth rate. If an investment goes from <em>initial</em> to <em>final</em> over n trading days (≈ n/252 years), the annualized rate r satisfies:
<br><code>final = initial × (1 + r)^(n/252)</code><br>
Solving for r: <code>r = (final/initial)^(252/n) − 1</code>. The exponent converts fractional years to an annual rate the way compounding works — it's the inverse of the compound interest formula.
<br><br>
<strong>APR_simple</strong> is just <code>ret.mean() × 252</code> — no exponentiation, just arithmetic scaling. APR assumes returns add; CAGR knows they multiply.
<br><br>
<strong>max(1.0, n):</strong> Guards against division-by-zero when n=0 (no return data). With n=1 there's also a corner case: one data point doesn't define a CAGR meaningfully. The <code>if len(eq) < 2</code> check above is the primary guard; <code>max(1.0, n)</code> is a belt-and-suspenders fallback.`
},

