{id:'wr-shrinkage', type:'wr', channel:'cov', channel_label:'Covariance & Portfolio Math',
 text:'What goes wrong when you directly invert a poorly-estimated sample covariance matrix? What does <strong>shrinkage toward the diagonal</strong> fix, structurally?',
 concepts:[
   {label:'Sample covariance needs many observations', terms:['sample','few','observation','n < p','undersampled','noisy']},
   {label:'Ill-conditioned / nearly singular', terms:['ill-condition','singular','invert','inverse','nearly singular','rank']},
   {label:'Small eigenvalues amplified by inversion', terms:['eigenvalue','amplif','inversion','blow','tiny','instab']},
   {label:'Shrinkage pulls toward diagonal', terms:['shrink','diagonal','blend','mix','stable','regulariz']},
   {label:'Lambda controls the tradeoff', terms:['lambda','lam','tradeoff','bias','variance','control']},
 ],
 answer:`<h4>Model Answer</h4>
<strong>The problem with raw inversion:</strong> The sample covariance matrix Σ_emp is estimated from N daily return rows and P assets. When N is not much larger than P (common in practice — 90 days of history for 50+ assets), the matrix is poorly conditioned. Its smallest eigenvalues are near-zero artifacts of estimation noise. Inverting Σ amplifies these tiny eigenvalues to huge values, so the portfolio weights blow up in directions that are pure noise — the optimizer confidently picks extreme positions that live entirely in the noise eigenvectors.
<br><br>
<strong>Shrinkage toward the diagonal:</strong> The formula <code>Σ = (1-λ)Σ_emp + λ·diag(Σ_emp)</code> blends the noisy full matrix with the stable diagonal. The diagonal (individual variances) is always well-conditioned because each diagonal entry only needs one asset's history. As λ → 1, off-diagonal correlations are discarded entirely; as λ → 0, the full sample estimate is used.
<br><br>
<strong>λ is a bias-variance tradeoff:</strong> More shrinkage = more bias (ignoring real correlations) but less variance (more stable inverse). The repo defaults to <code>--shrink 0.2</code>, keeping most of the estimated correlations while adding a regularizing nudge.`
},

{id:'wr-meanvar', type:'wr', channel:'cov', channel_label:'Covariance & Portfolio Math',
 text:'Explain how <code>predict_next.py:meanvar_long_only</code> computes portfolio weights. What is the unconstrained mean-variance solution, and what does the <code>max_w</code> cap add?',
 concepts:[
   {label:'Unconstrained MV weights: w ∝ Σ⁻¹μ', terms:['sigma inverse','σ⁻¹','pinv','inverse','mu','expected return','proportional']},
   {label:'Long-only: clip negatives to zero', terms:['long-only','clip','negative','zero','np.clip','positive']},
   {label:'Normalize to sum to 1', terms:['normalize','sum to 1','divide by sum','w_sum','total weight']},
   {label:'max_w cap on individual positions', terms:['max_w','cap','0.3','maximum','individual','position size']},
 ],
 answer:`<h4>Model Answer</h4>
The unconstrained mean-variance solution (Markowitz 1952) is:
<br><code>w* ∝ Σ⁻¹μ</code>
where μ is the vector of expected (predicted) returns and Σ is the covariance matrix. This weight vector maximizes the Sharpe ratio.
<br><br>
In <code>meanvar_long_only</code>:
<ol style="margin:.5rem 0 .5rem 1.2rem;line-height:1.8">
  <li><code>w = np.linalg.pinv(Sigma_reg) @ mu</code> — compute Σ⁻¹μ using pseudoinverse (handles near-singular Σ).</li>
  <li><code>w = np.clip(w, 0, None)</code> — set negative weights to zero (long-only constraint).</li>
  <li><code>w = w / w.sum()</code> — normalize to sum to 1 (fully invested).</li>
  <li><code>w = np.clip(w, 0, max_w)</code> then renormalize — cap each position at max_w=30% to prevent over-concentration.</li>
</ol>
<code>Sigma_reg = Sigma + eps × I</code> is a small ridge term to prevent exact singularity before inversion.`
},

