{id:'wr-alpha-vs-sharpe', type:'wr', channel:'capm', channel_label:'Alpha, Beta & CAPM',
 text:'<strong>Jensen\'s alpha</strong> and the <strong>Sharpe ratio</strong> are both performance metrics, but they measure different things. Explain what each captures, when a strategy might have high Sharpe but zero alpha, and vice versa.',
 concepts:[
   {label:'Sharpe measures return per unit of total risk', terms:['sharpe','total risk','volatility','per unit','risk-adjusted']},
   {label:'Alpha measures market-adjusted return', terms:['alpha','market-adjusted','beta','capm','residual','unexplained']},
   {label:'High Sharpe, zero alpha: market exposure', terms:['high sharpe zero alpha','leveraged beta','beta exposure','market','replicate']},
   {label:'Positive alpha, low Sharpe: high noise', terms:['positive alpha low sharpe','noisy','volatile','high vol','t-stat']},
 ],
 answer:`<h4>Model Answer</h4>
<strong>Sharpe ratio</strong> = (annualized return − r_f) / annualized volatility. It measures return per unit of <em>total</em> volatility, regardless of where that return comes from. It doesn't distinguish skill from market exposure.
<br><br>
<strong>Jensen's alpha</strong> = the CAPM regression intercept: the return the strategy earns <em>after</em> subtracting β × market return. Pure alpha can't be replicated by buying index ETFs.
<br><br>
<strong>High Sharpe, zero alpha:</strong> A strategy that invests 1.3× in SPY (β=1.3, α=0) can have a high Sharpe ratio if the market Sharpe is high. The Sharpe is rewarding market exposure, not skill. Anyone can do this.
<br><br>
<strong>Positive alpha, low Sharpe:</strong> A strategy might have genuine market-neutral skill (α > 0) but also high idiosyncratic volatility, producing a low Sharpe. The alpha is real but noisy — the t-stat of α is what matters, not the raw value.`
},

{id:'wr-beta-hedge-ratio', type:'wr', channel:'capm', channel_label:'Alpha, Beta & CAPM',
 text:'Lesson 0028 noted that the <code>hedge_ratio</code> in <code>solutions/03_stat_arb_risk/pairs.py</code> and the CAPM <strong>beta</strong> are computed with the same OLS formula. Explain the structural similarity. What is different between them?',
 concepts:[
   {label:'Both are OLS regression slopes', terms:['ols','regression','slope','beta hat','least squares','lstsq']},
   {label:'Both are cov(x,y)/var(x)', terms:['cov','var','covariance','variance','ratio','formula']},
   {label:'Different x: spread vs market', terms:['spread','pairs','cointegration','market return','different x','regressand']},
   {label:'Different purpose: hedging vs attribution', terms:['hedge','attribution','purpose','use','stat arb','pairs']},
 ],
 answer:`<h4>Model Answer</h4>
<strong>Both are OLS slopes:</strong> β = cov(y, x) / var(x) = (XᵀX)⁻¹Xᵀy for a one-feature OLS.
<br><br>
<strong>hedge_ratio in pairs.py:</strong> y = asset A returns, x = asset B returns. The slope tells you how many shares of B to short per share of A to make the spread stationary.
<br><br>
<strong>Market beta:</strong> y = strategy returns, x = market (SPY) returns. The slope tells you the strategy's exposure to broad market moves.
<br><br>
<strong>Structurally identical math, different interpretation:</strong>
<ul style="margin:.5rem 0 .5rem 1.2rem;line-height:1.8">
  <li>Hedge ratio → use it to construct a spread portfolio</li>
  <li>Beta → use it to measure/attribute performance, or to hedge the market out of a strategy</li>
</ul>
In fact, a long-short pairs trade with a proper hedge ratio <em>is</em> market-neutral because by construction the pair's beta to the market is near zero.`
},

{id:'wr-capm-decomp', type:'wr', channel:'capm', channel_label:'Alpha, Beta & CAPM',
 text:'CAPM decomposes strategy returns as: <strong>r_s = α + β·r_m + ε</strong>. Explain what each term represents, and how you would estimate this using the existing tools in this repo.',
